-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell D23 (S1-NS (a), DEFINER twin of I19): this module DEFINES its own
-- `size` and reaches the interface ONLY by naming a SIBLING method. `import ifc.{tag}`
-- admits `tag` and nothing else, so `size` is not in S1's right operand here, it is not a
-- shadow, and the module's OWN top-level binding answers: 42.
--
-- 🚨 THIS IS THE S0 THE WHOLE S1-NS PASS IS ABOUT, AND IT HAD NO FIXTURE AT ALL. Under
-- per-ROW admission the interface was admitted because a DIFFERENT method was named, `size`
-- became a shadow, and S2's definer inversion never got the chance to fire -- so a module's
-- own top-level function was silently replaced by a foreign impl body, printing 777 at exit
-- 0 with no diagnostic on any verb. A module losing its own definition to an interface it
-- never named is the worst shape in this corpus, and the corpus could not express it.
--
-- The receiver is String, inside the standalone's domain AND at the impl's head, so both
-- candidates typecheck: only a pinned VALUE can tell 42 from 777. The definer/importer pair
-- matters because S2 treats the two kinds differently -- I19 is the importer half, and a fix
-- that repaired one without the other would leave this one silent.
import ifc.{tag}

size : String -> Int
size s = 42

main = println (size "abc")
